# BluFi module Kconfig
menuconfig BLUFI
	bool "BluFi BLE provisioning protocol (ESP BluFi compatible)"
	default n
	select BT
	select BT_PERIPHERAL
	select BT_DRIVERS
	select BT_ESP32
	help
	  Reusable BluFi protocol implementation: the frame codec plus the GATT
	  transport and security handshake on Zephyr BLE, compatible with the Espressif
	  apps and the WeChat mini-program provisioning flow.

if BLUFI

config BLUFI_STRICT_SEQ
	bool "Reject a frame whose sequence number is out of order"
	default y
	help
	  ESP-IDF's receiver tracks recv_seq, refuses a frame whose seq is not the expected
	  next one, and reports SEQUENCE_ERROR on DATA/ERROR_INFO. On (the default) matches
	  that, so a phone that sent a frame twice, or a frame that arrived out of order, is
	  told so instead of being acted on twice.

	  Off accepts the frame anyway, at the cost of diverging from upstream: only worth
	  it while debugging a client that restarts its sequence without reconnecting.

config BLUFI_SECURITY
	bool "BluFi security handshake (DH + AES-CFB128 + MD5)"
	default n
	help
	  Enables the ESP-compatible BluFi key negotiation (Diffie-Hellman) and
	  AES-128-CFB128 payload encryption used when the phone app selects
	  encrypted provisioning.

	  The algorithms are not part of this repository: they are the Mbed TLS
	  2.28 LTS sources checked out by the west manifest (dhm, bignum, aes and
	  md5, unmodified; AES with its symbols renamed at compile time because the
	  application already links an AES of its own). If that module is not
	  present the build fails with instructions rather than silently compiling a
	  copy - point -DBLUFI_MBEDTLS_DIR at a checkout if your layout differs.

	  Left off by default: plaintext provisioning interoperates with both the
	  Espressif apps and the WeChat mini-program, and what this handshake adds is
	  protocol compatibility rather than strength - its DH group is 256 bits and
	  the key is MD5-derived.

config BLUFI_BLE_SUPERVISION_TIMEOUT
	int "Supervision timeout to request for the link (10 ms units, 0 = central's choice)"
	default 0
	depends on BLUFI
	help
	  A Wi-Fi scan or association burst deafens the ESP32's shared 2.4 GHz
	  radio to BLE for seconds; against centrals that negotiated a short
	  supervision timeout the link then drops (reason 8, supervision
	  timeout) mid-provisioning and the phone never receives the final
	  report. Set this (600 = 6 s, the Apple guideline maximum) to request
	  a timeout that survives the radio gap; the central may still refuse.
	  0 keeps whatever the central negotiated.

endif
